---
description: Resolve bugs and quality gaps at their general owning cause
alwaysApply: true
---

# Root-cause resolution

Every bug, bottleneck, quality gap, recurring pain point, or unexpected outcome is evidence of a
system mechanism that permitted it. Remove that mechanism at its owning boundary; never patch only
the symptom or specialize a fix to the scene that exposed it.

- Reproduce and trace the full causal chain: outcome, enabling condition, agent/runtime decision,
  missing evidence or context, and the boundary that should have rejected it.
- Classify the cause: implementation, architecture, reasoning, prompt/context, plan/contract,
  instrument, tool policy, validation, or authority/provenance. Name all contributing boundaries.
- Fix the earliest owning cause. Reject downstream guards, retries, tolerance changes, broadened
  permissions, prompt reminders, and fixture-specific branches that only hide recurrence.
- The root cause is the earliest decision or reasoning step that made the failure reachable —
  an ADR/HIR assumption, plan/contract decision, ownership boundary, or an agent reasoning step
  the harness allowed — not the code where it surfaced. Fix at that step; supersede a recorded
  decision and migrate rather than coding around its consequences.
- Investigate reasoning failures as missing measurements, choices, bounded context, or read-back
  before changing prompts. Mechanical failures require mechanical enforcement.
- Record how the system made the failure representable; identify mechanisms, not blame.
- Never encode the exposing shot's names, objects, coordinates, frames, layer count, vocabulary,
  or data layout. The exposing scene proves reproduction, not generality.
- Prove the fix with heterogeneous fixtures, an injected failure, and a regression test asserting
  the general invariant. Preserve unrelated behavior.
- If a permanent scene-independent fix is out of scope, stop and escalate with evidence. Do not
  commit a workaround.
